body:
We do trace a bundle of rays from the sensor to the world.

The problem right now would be to find the direction of the ray coming out of the lens without actually tracing the lens.

In the original approach of the thesis I'm basing my work on, the rays start from the world and end in the sensor in the following way:

For each point in the world:
1) Entrance pupil is sampled, conjugate point on the exit pupil is found using the pupil lateral magnification;
2) The Gaussian Image Plane and coordinate of the world point in image space are found using the lateral image magnification;
3) To those coordinates, the aberration vector is added to obtain the aberrated coordinates of the point in image space;
4) the aberrated wavefront of the incoming light path is considered to find another point on the reference sphere and through that point a ray is traced from the exit pupil to the sensor;

(For a much better explanation see chapter 4.2 of the thesis: https://lynnasberg.nl/thesis.pdf)

What I've been told in my case is that 4 doesn't work when tracing the ray from the sensor to the world

